In $\mathbb{R}^3,$ there are exactly two vectors orthogonal to a pair of vectors in $\mathbb{R}^3.$  They are scalar multiples of each other, so the direction is determined (up to a scalar factor) and can be represented by a unit vector.  The cross product is defined as a way to switch from a direction to a vector of unit magnitude.  In other words, given $\mathbf{v}$ the direction of the vector, $\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0},$ so $\frac{\mathbf{v}}{|\mathbf{v}|}$ is the direction of the vector $\mathbf{v}.$

Then we have that
\[\frac{\mathbf{v}}{|\mathbf{v}|} \times \frac{\mathbf{w}}{|\mathbf{w}|} = \frac{\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{w}}{|\mathbf{v}| |\mathbf{w}|}.\]Therefore,
\begin{align*}
